# `pricing::HestonCfMode`

Which algebraic form of the Heston characteristic function the Fourier engines evaluate.

The characteristic function of the log-asset contains a complex square root and logarithm whose branch can jump at long maturities, producing a discontinuity in the integrand. `Original` is Heston's 1993 form; `LittleTrap` is the rearrangement that is continuous in the branch (Albrecher et al.), and is the default and the safe choice. At moderate maturities the two agree.

## Fields

### `Original`

`enumerator`, default `0`.

Heston's original form.

### `LittleTrap`

`enumerator`, default `1`.

The branch-continuous 'little Heston trap' form. The default.

## Guidance

Set as `Fft::mode`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The two forms at one year agree.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(10));
  const auto inst = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0);
  const auto mkt = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0);
  const auto mdl = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}).as<fin::model::Heston>();

  for (const auto mode : {fin::pricing::HestonCfMode::Original, fin::pricing::HestonCfMode::LittleTrap}) {
    fin::pricer::Fft e;
    e.mode = mode;
    out.writeln(static_cast<int>(mode), ":", e.price(inst, mdl, mkt).price.items<double>()[0]);
  }
}
```

### Example 2

At a long maturity with a strong volatility of variance the original form overflows, and the engine refuses it with advice instead of returning NaN; the branch-continuous form prices. A Monte Carlo price (quadratic-exponential scheme, 200,000 paths) and the cosine engine confirm the stable form.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto inst = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 10.0);
  const auto mkt = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0);
  const auto mdl = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {0.5, 0.04, 1.0, -0.9, 0.04}).as<fin::model::Heston>();
  for (const auto mode : {fin::pricing::HestonCfMode::Original, fin::pricing::HestonCfMode::LittleTrap}) {
    fin::pricer::Fft e;
    e.mode = mode;
    try {
      out.writeln(mode == fin::pricing::HestonCfMode::Original ? "Original  " : "LittleTrap", ":", e.price(inst, mdl, mkt).price.items<double>()[0]);
    } catch (const std::exception &error) {
      const std::string text(error.what());
      out.writeln("Original  : refused:", text.substr(text.find("::", 20) + 2, 95), "...");
    }
  }
  fin::pricer::COS cos;
  out.writeln("COS       :", cos.price(inst, mdl, mkt).price.items<double>()[0]);
  fin::pricer::MonteCarlo mc;
  mc.scheme = fin::volatility::SimScheme::QuadExp;
  mc.paths = 200000;
  mc.steps = 200;
  mc.seed = 5;
  out.writeln("Monte Carlo:", mc.price(inst, mdl, mkt).price.items<double>()[0]);
}
```

### Example 3

Through a Field.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(8));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Fft, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};

  field.controls.fft.mode = fin::pricing::HestonCfMode::Original;
  out.writeln("Original:", field.price().price.items<double>()[0]);
}
```

